<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide MSBuild defaults. There is no .sln here and the four projects build
    independently, so this file is their only shared configuration point.

    Boundary: only properties that should be identical across all projects. Anything
    specific to a single project stays in its own csproj — don't turn this into a
    second dumping ground.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Single source of truth for the product version.
         Before this, 0.1.6 was hand-copied into three csproj files (App / Cli / Avalonia),
         while Core and Checks carried no Version at all — the result was a single build
         artifact where Core.dll reported 1.0.0 and Cli.dll reported 0.1.6.
         installer/Build.ps1 and installer/Test.ps1 also read the version from this file;
         stop copying extra copies anywhere else. -->
    <Version>0.9.0</Version>

    <!-- The NuGet-side equivalent of the repo's existing "version + SHA-256 double pin"
         philosophy: pin each project's full transitive closure in packages.lock.json so
         different dev machines don't resolve different versions.

         Spell out the boundary, don't oversell it: what this fixes is **version drift**,
         not tampering — NuGet global packages already carry SHA-512 checks, and a lock
         file is not a signature.

         **Only projects with real dependencies keep a lock file**: a lock file's value
         comes from "pinning package versions". The lock file of a zero-dependency project
         contains nothing but empty framework/RID nodes — it pins nothing, yet still jitters
         along with the RID behavior below. Today App is the only project in the repo with a
         PackageReference, so the repo **tracks App's lock file only**; Core / Cli / Checks
         each turn this property off explicitly in their csproj (delete that line when the
         first PackageReference is added).

         RestoreLockedMode is deliberately not enabled: this machine only has SDK 10, so the
         net8.0 targeting pack is restored through NuGet here, while CI's SDK 8 takes it from
         the directory shipped with the SDK — the two can produce lock files with different
         content, and locking outright would make CI fail lock-file validation. The order is:
         commit the lock file first → wait for CI to genuinely go green once → then decide
         whether to switch to locked mode. (Also: CI currently neither enables locked-mode nor
         runs an explicit dotnet restore, so the lock file is not yet a gate.)

         **Lock files jitter, and that is deterministic NuGet behavior, not an environment
         anomaly** (reproduced by measurement on 2026-10-04): the content depends on whether
         the last restore passed -r —
           · without a RID: writes "the base TFM node + one node per declared RuntimeIdentifiers";
           · with -r <rid>: **writes only that single RID node and prunes the rest**, and it
             **rewrites the lock files of referenced projects along the way** (restoring App
             with -r win-x64 adds net8.0/win-x64 to Core, even though Core itself declares
             no RID at all).
         installer/Build.ps1 runs dotnet publish -r win-x64, so **every local package build
         prunes App's lock file from multiple RIDs down to 1** (Core's copy is no longer
         tracked, so it is unaffected).
         Don't count on "declaring RuntimeIdentifiers settles everything": that holds for
         Cli but not for App, because a single -r restore still prunes multiple RIDs down to
         one. **Running a RID-less dotnet restore once before committing restores it**
         (measured: after one restore per project across the four projects, git status goes
         back to zero). -->
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
